---

<BaseLayout
  title="Kayoko Mimura - Honorary Head Instructor"
  description="Profile of the late Kayoko Mimura, second head instructor and honorary head instructor of Seishinkan Dojo, Seishinkai Headquarters, All Japan Seishinkai Karate Federation."
  lang="en"
  path="/instructors/mimura/"
>
  <section class="dojo-list">
    <div class="page-container">

      <div class="section__title-area">
        <span class="section-label">PROFILE</span>
        <h1 class="section-title">Kayoko Mimura</h1>
      </div>

      <div class="profile-detail">

        <p class="profile-detail__role">
          Second Head Instructor, Seishinkan Dojo, Seishinkai Headquarters
          (Honorary Head Instructor)<br />
          1974.6.11 - 2015.11.27
        </p>

        <h2 class="detail-page__heading">Profile</h2>

        <div class="profile-detail__body">
          <div class="detail-page__img-text-pair--reverse">

            <div class="detail-media--med fade-right-in">
              <figure>
                <img
                  src="/assets/images/instructors/mimura/img_mimura_fv.jpg"
                  alt="Kayoko Mimura"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </figure>
            </div>

            <div>

              <p>
                After graduating from Hokusei Gakuen Women's Junior College
                with a degree in English, she began studying Seishinryu Karate
                at the Yomiuri Culture School while working as an office
                worker.<br />

                She became the second trainee in the training program and was
                appointed head of the Sakaemachi Branch, completing the program
                in 2006. During an interview with Doshi Sports, she spoke of
                her dream of becoming a "female head instructor" in the future.<br />

                In 2008, she underwent a total gastrectomy due to stage IV
                stomach cancer. That same year, she became the first full-time
                employee of the NPO Seishinkai.<br />

                In 2009, she was appointed Shihan (Master Instructor).
              </p>

              <p>
                Through her daily dedication to training, she became widely
                recognized as the Chairman's top student and successor. In
                2012, she was appointed the second Head Instructor of Seishinkai
                Headquarters. She held the rank of Renshi 6th Dan in
                Seishinryu Karate-do.<br />

                She was a pioneer among female instructors in Seishinkai
                Karate-do, becoming Seishinkai's first female Shihan and
                female Head Instructor.<br />

                Fluent in English and capable of simultaneous interpretation,
                she served as Director of the International Department and
                hosted karate enthusiasts from overseas who visited Japan
                each year. In 2013, she traveled to Sri Lanka to teach karate.<br />

                In 2015, she was awarded Kyoshi 7th Dan. In addition to karate,
                she studied Okinawan Kobudo (weapons arts) and Iaido.<br />

                She served as Executive Director of the NPO All Japan
                Seishinkai and as Head Instructor and Director of the
                International Department at ISKF Headquarters, helping to build
                the foundation of Seishinkai.
              </p>

              <p>
                On November 27, 2015, her cancer returned and she passed away
                at a young age. She was posthumously awarded 9th Dan in honor
                of her achievements. More than 800 members and others attended
                her funeral.<br />

                Her contributions are honored through her title of Honorary
                Head Instructor.<br />

                In 2016, a memorial monument and Seishinkai commemorative
                monument were erected at Fujino Seizan-en. Every year, memorial
                visits are held three times: during
                <a
                  href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kbx1rgE_Gec"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                >Golden Week</a>,
                <a
                  href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CskzxHmgsk8"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                >Obon</a>, and
                <a
                  href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXWBOzv3jxs"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                >November</a>.
              </p>

            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ContentSlider
          slides={mimuraSlides}
          ratio=""
          autoplaySeconds={10}
          class="content-slider--history"
        />

        <section class="partner-section">
          <h2 class="detail-page__heading">
            Kayoko Mimura in Videos
          </h2>

          <div class="youtube-grid">
            {
              memorialVideos.map((video) => (
                <a
                  class="youtube-card"
                  href={video.href}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                >
                  <span class="youtube-card__thumb">
                    <img
                      src={video.image}
                      alt={`${video.caption} (YouTube)`}
                      loading="lazy"
                    />
                  </span>
                  <span>{video.caption}</span>
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </section>

        <p class="detail-page__back">
          <a href="/instructors/">← Back to Instructors & Branch Heads</a>
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>